WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.620
Picture this. You've got an AI assistant scanning your shared company drive and it doesn't open a

00:05.620 --> 00:09.560
single document. Like it doesn't read a single line of text. It doesn't parse a single spreadsheet.

00:09.860 --> 00:16.400
Nothing. Just scanning the surface. Exactly. But suddenly it starts generating all these mandatory

00:16.400 --> 00:21.260
tasks, assigning them to your team members and basically just clogging up everyone's calendar

00:21.260 --> 00:25.900
for the week. Which is a nightmare. A total nightmare. And you're wondering why did this

00:25.900 --> 00:31.820
happen? Well, simply because it saw the words incomplete report in a file name. It didn't

00:31.820 --> 00:37.180
analyze the context. It didn't know what was inside. It just saw a title and launched right

00:37.180 --> 00:43.060
into action. Which is such a common thing now. The beauty of that little anecdote, which is where

00:43.060 --> 00:48.280
we're starting today, is that it really exposes this massive vulnerability in how we currently

00:48.280 --> 00:54.360
interact with digital environments. Oh, absolutely. So today's mission for this deep dive is to take

00:54.360 --> 00:58.660
that terrifying little AI hallucination and completely flip it on its head. I love that.

00:58.800 --> 01:04.080
We are looking at this brilliant September 2026 paper by the independent researcher Fleijan.

01:04.240 --> 01:10.160
It's titled File Names as a Deliberately Built Berm. Such a great concept. Right. And we are going

01:10.160 --> 01:17.940
to explore how you can turn that exact AI accident into this deliberate ironclad productivity method

01:17.940 --> 01:23.620
for yourself. Because, I mean, we tend to think of files and folders as just these static storage

01:23.620 --> 01:29.020
boxes, right? Yeah, like digital cardboard boxes. Exactly. But the moment you introduce autonomous

01:29.020 --> 01:34.280
agents, or honestly even just your sleep-deprived co-workers who are quickly scanning a directory

01:34.280 --> 01:40.980
at 5 p.m., those file names, they cease to be just labels. They become instructions. Yes,

01:41.080 --> 01:47.300
they become executable instructions. Nothing inside that file actually asked for a task to be created.

01:47.300 --> 01:54.620
The title alone basically hijacked the AI's logic. So if stale labels and messy file names are,

01:54.980 --> 01:59.560
you know, the digital equivalent of paving a path right off a cliff, then we really can't just let

01:59.560 --> 02:03.880
people use whatever words they want to name their files. No, it's way too dangerous now.

02:04.520 --> 02:10.740
So today we're unpacking how using a strict 10-word vocabulary in your file names can turn a

02:10.740 --> 02:16.580
completely chaotic folder into a self-updating status board. And the best part is it requires

02:16.580 --> 02:22.740
no database. None. No complex project management software either. It just seamlessly guides both

02:22.740 --> 02:28.760
human workers and AI agents. But, you know, to really wrap our heads around why that AI got so

02:28.760 --> 02:34.780
confused by the words incomplete report, we have to break down the underlying economics of a file system

02:34.780 --> 02:41.660
first. The economics, like how much it costs to do something. Exactly. We have to look at how a

02:41.660 --> 02:49.380
computer or an LLM or even a human brain calculates the actual biological and computational cost of

02:49.380 --> 02:56.040
reading digital information. Okay, let's pull that apart. So if I open a folder on my desktop right now,

02:56.300 --> 03:01.900
my operating system is making choices about what to render first, right? What does that hierarchy of

03:01.900 --> 03:08.120
reading costs actually look like? Well, Flyxion defines a very strict hierarchy here.

03:08.540 --> 03:14.520
And it's all rooted in operating system fundamentals. At the absolute bottom, the cheapest thing you can

03:14.520 --> 03:21.180
possibly read is a directory listing. The file names. Just the file names. When you type like

03:21.180 --> 03:27.740
LS in a terminal or you open a finder window, your OS doesn't actually open any of the files.

03:27.740 --> 03:33.600
Right. It just shows you what's there. Yeah, it just reads the directory node. And that node contains

03:33.600 --> 03:40.840
this super lightweight list of names and pointers. It is returned instantly, all at once, with virtually

03:40.840 --> 03:47.940
zero computational overhead. Wow. Okay. So every tool that touches a computer, your shell, your file

03:47.940 --> 03:55.620
manager, your cloud drive's web view, and crucially, an AI agent's very first exploratory call to a system.

03:55.620 --> 03:59.420
They all consume this surface first. Exactly. Because it's free.

03:59.940 --> 04:04.200
It's basically the digital equivalent of glancing at the spine of a book on a shelf. Like,

