18:23.660 --> 18:27.500
renaming a file can sometimes completely confuse the heuristics.

18:28.040 --> 18:30.540
Right, because Git doesn't actually track files.

18:30.660 --> 18:31.440
It tracks content.

18:31.780 --> 18:32.240
Exactly.

18:32.680 --> 18:38.840
When you rename a file, Git uses a similarity index to figure out if you deleted file A and

18:38.840 --> 18:42.960
created a completely new file B, or if you just renamed file A.

18:43.580 --> 18:45.940
Oh, so if you change too much, it gets lost.

18:46.340 --> 18:46.680
Yes.

18:47.220 --> 18:52.900
If you change the status prefix and you tweak the title even a little bit, the similarity

18:52.900 --> 18:54.780
index might drop below the threshold.

18:55.120 --> 18:56.060
And then what happens?

18:56.060 --> 19:01.080
The system thinks the old file is dead, and a completely unrelated new file was born.

19:01.620 --> 19:04.220
You lose the entire historical context of the document.

19:04.220 --> 19:06.700
You can't see who worked on it last month.

19:07.300 --> 19:08.980
That's a disaster for a team project.

19:09.360 --> 19:16.280
But by keeping the title exactly the same, buildproofofconcept.md, and only changing the

19:16.280 --> 19:23.180
prefix from open to verify, Git perfectly recognizes that this is the exact same file undergoing

19:23.180 --> 19:23.800
a rename.

19:24.280 --> 19:26.100
So it preserves the entire history.

19:26.460 --> 19:30.800
It is a very elegant concession to the underlying mechanics of modern version control.

19:30.800 --> 19:35.380
But there is a second rule that creates, well, a bit of friction for the user.

19:35.760 --> 19:37.040
The header line rule.

19:37.380 --> 19:38.220
Yes, the header line.

19:38.580 --> 19:43.640
Flyxion states that the very first line inside the document must restate the status and list

19:43.640 --> 19:44.360
any dependencies.

19:44.880 --> 19:45.240
Correct.

19:45.240 --> 19:51.620
So the file name says, blocked apply re-knotting model.md, and the first line inside the actual

19:51.620 --> 19:55.920
text file says, status, blocked, depends, buildproofofconcept.

19:56.420 --> 19:56.740
Right.

19:57.260 --> 20:00.660
Doesn't that violate the dry principle, you know, don't repeat yourself?

20:01.500 --> 20:04.440
Why am I doing double-entry bookkeeping for my personal notes?

20:04.840 --> 20:10.000
Like, I barely want to name the file once, let alone open it up and type the exact same

20:10.000 --> 20:11.240
status at the top of the page?

20:11.560 --> 20:12.560
I get that complaint.

20:13.140 --> 20:15.640
It absolutely is a deliberate compromise.

20:15.880 --> 20:18.180
And yes, it requires discipline.

20:18.680 --> 20:21.320
But consider the alternative engineering implementation.

20:21.600 --> 20:22.380
Which would be what?

20:22.380 --> 20:28.160
What if you tried to pack all that dependency metadata into the file name itself to avoid

20:28.160 --> 20:28.960
repeating yourself?

20:29.580 --> 20:35.620
You'd end up with a file name like, blocked, waiting on proof of concept, apply re-knotting

20:35.620 --> 20:36.940
model.md.

20:36.940 --> 20:37.560
Oh, yuck.

20:37.640 --> 20:39.500
Which is completely unreadable for a human.

20:39.840 --> 20:42.840
And it completely breaks the stable title rule.

20:43.080 --> 20:43.440
Right.

20:43.660 --> 20:48.040
Because every time a dependency changes or is cleared, the entire file name has to shift.

20:48.400 --> 20:51.600
And that might break hyperlinks from other documents pointing to it.

20:51.800 --> 20:52.080
Okay.

20:52.220 --> 20:52.840
That makes sense.

20:53.580 --> 20:58.560
By putting the dependencies inside the file on line one, you keep the file name doing

20:58.560 --> 21:04.640
its one singular job perfectly, answering the question, what may be done to this file right

21:04.640 --> 21:04.960
now?

21:05.180 --> 21:05.620
Exactly.

21:06.220 --> 21:11.060
Yes, checking the dependencies costs one system open because you have to read the first

21:11.060 --> 21:11.760
line of the file.

21:11.760 --> 21:15.680
But it protects the integrity and the brevity of that nearest surface.

21:16.360 --> 21:16.380
Okay.

21:16.520 --> 21:21.000
So to prove this isn't all just abstract academic theory, the author actually provides the code

21:21.000 --> 21:21.960
for a tool in the paper.

21:22.160 --> 21:23.200
A very cool little tool.

21:23.400 --> 21:26.240
A lightweight seven-command Python script called Berm.

21:26.240 --> 21:31.420
And it shows how this simple grammar turns a dumb directory of text files into a living,

21:31.720 --> 21:32.540
breathing database.

21:32.780 --> 21:35.800
And this is where this digmergic Berm truly comes to life.

21:36.200 --> 21:39.220
Because the script doesn't use a massive backend SQL database.

21:39.720 --> 21:42.480
It doesn't require a cloud subscription or an internet connection.

21:42.820 --> 21:48.200
It literally just runs os.lister to read the file names and occasionally opens a file to

21:48.200 --> 21:48.840
read the first line.

21:49.060 --> 21:50.320
It's brilliantly simple.

21:50.820 --> 21:53.080
Let's walk through what this tool can actually do.

21:53.600 --> 21:53.780
Okay.

21:53.780 --> 21:56.300
There's a board command, which just groups everything by status.

21:56.300 --> 21:59.820
It gives you an instant Kanban board right in your terminal output.

22:00.380 --> 22:00.680
Very handy.

22:01.040 --> 22:05.440
There's a next command, which scans the directory, opens only the files that look like candidates

22:05.440 --> 22:09.700
based on their cheap file names, confirms their internal headers, and tells you exactly

22:09.700 --> 22:11.020
what is ready to be worked on.

22:11.260 --> 22:15.820
And crucially, that next command identifies blocked items whose dependencies have suddenly

22:15.820 --> 22:16.320
been met.

22:16.540 --> 22:18.540
Oh, so it automates the unblocking process.

22:18.760 --> 22:19.020
Yes.

22:19.020 --> 22:22.280
You don't have to constantly check if the person before you finished.

22:22.860 --> 22:27.120
There's also stale, which finds disagreements between the file name and the header.

22:27.480 --> 22:30.240
A lifesaver for catching those time-traveling files.

22:30.820 --> 22:33.620
And move, which is like a quality of life macro.

22:33.840 --> 22:38.180
It updates the file name and the internal header simultaneously so you don't forget to do one

22:38.180 --> 22:38.660
or the other.

22:38.840 --> 22:39.040
Right.

22:39.040 --> 22:44.500
But the one that really blew my mind, the one that perfectly proves the theory of stigmargy

22:44.500 --> 22:46.300
was the history command.

22:46.300 --> 22:51.600
Oh, the history command is a perfect illustration of emergent behavior in a complex system.

22:52.460 --> 22:54.920
The paper runs a simulation on a 13-file roadmap.

22:55.480 --> 23:00.900
One of the files is a proof of concept that moves from open to experiment to verify to extend

23:00.900 --> 23:01.920
over the course of three weeks.

23:02.160 --> 23:02.340
Right.

23:02.340 --> 23:08.000
And when you run the Berm history script, it generates this gorgeous, perfectly formatted

23:08.000 --> 23:08.640
project log.

23:08.980 --> 23:13.540
It prints out exactly when the file changed states, how long it sat in verification, and

23:13.540 --> 23:14.240
who moved it.

23:14.620 --> 23:19.220
And I realized while reading this, no one actually sat down and wrote that log.

23:19.320 --> 23:19.620
No one.

23:19.840 --> 23:22.240
No project manager was updating a JIRA ticket.

23:22.400 --> 23:23.860
No one was taking meeting minutes.

23:24.240 --> 23:30.420
The project log is simply a byproduct of everyone on the team renaming files for their own selfish

23:30.420 --> 23:34.100
local reasons, just to keep their own immediate work organized.

23:34.440 --> 23:35.360
That is so cool.

23:35.360 --> 23:41.660
No one is acting as a centralized project manager, yet the system produces perfect project management

23:41.660 --> 23:45.540
documentation automatically, just by reading the git rename history.

23:45.900 --> 23:46.920
It's like the termites.

23:47.400 --> 23:48.720
Exactly like the termites.

23:49.360 --> 23:50.720
Termites don't draw blueprints.

23:50.960 --> 23:52.460
They just follow local rules.

23:53.020 --> 23:56.940
You just follow the local rule of renaming your file, and the blueprint emerges.

23:56.940 --> 24:02.800
In the simulation, there's a specific output where the tool prints, unblock, apply renodding

24:02.800 --> 24:03.900
model to S star.

24:04.200 --> 24:04.420
Right.

24:04.860 --> 24:08.840
The tool noticed that this blocked file was waiting on a dependency, and it noticed that

24:08.840 --> 24:11.500
the dependency had finally reached the extend status.

24:11.900 --> 24:15.000
So it automatically surfaced, the blocked task is ready to go.

24:15.360 --> 24:17.540
Nobody had to remember to check if the blocker was cleared.

24:17.780 --> 24:19.600
The environment just handled it.

24:19.840 --> 24:23.060
But, you know, we have to look at the cold, hard math.

24:23.060 --> 24:23.460
Right.

24:23.860 --> 24:27.340
We know the theory is elegant, but how much time does this actually save you?

24:27.660 --> 24:30.780
And more importantly, where does the system completely break down?

24:31.180 --> 24:33.280
Because every system has a breaking point.

24:34.000 --> 24:38.860
Flyxion ran a robust simulation to measure the exact computational savings and the failure

24:38.860 --> 24:39.200
rates.

24:39.500 --> 24:43.800
They generated a corpus of 60 items with a realistic mix of statuses.

24:43.900 --> 24:44.080
Yes.

24:44.360 --> 24:48.020
Then they tested three different file management strategies for answering questions like,

24:48.240 --> 24:50.820
you know, what needs checking or what can I build on?

24:50.820 --> 24:52.920
Let's break down those three strategies.

24:53.140 --> 24:53.260
Okay.

24:53.600 --> 24:55.460
First, there's the opaque strategy.

24:55.580 --> 24:57.440
This is how 99% of people work today.

24:57.660 --> 24:58.000
Absolutely.

24:58.440 --> 25:00.340
The file names don't carry any status.

25:00.480 --> 25:02.980
They're just named things like data model V2.

25:03.400 --> 25:08.200
So to figure out what needs checking, you have to physically open all 60 files, read the contents,

25:08.360 --> 25:09.120
and judge their state.

25:09.260 --> 25:10.280
The cost is maximum.

25:10.500 --> 25:14.460
60 files, 60 opens, 60 reads, 60 judgments.

25:15.260 --> 25:18.960
It is an order and operation where the constant factor is incredibly high.

25:19.120 --> 25:19.580
Super slow.

25:19.580 --> 25:21.860
Then there's the trust strategy.

25:22.160 --> 25:26.160
This is where you use the 10-word vocabulary, but you trust the file names blindly.

25:26.540 --> 25:26.560
Right.

25:26.740 --> 25:27.700
You act on the names alone.

25:27.800 --> 25:29.660
So you open zero files to search.

25:29.780 --> 25:33.500
You just run a search for the prefix verify and immediately act on the results.

25:33.760 --> 25:34.820
Which is incredibly fast.

25:34.980 --> 25:36.100
Near zero cost.

25:36.740 --> 25:39.720
But highly vulnerable to those stale labels we talked about.

25:40.400 --> 25:42.880
And finally, there's the indexed strategy.

25:43.200 --> 25:45.060
This is what the Bermscript actually uses.

25:45.360 --> 25:46.240
How does that one work?

25:46.240 --> 25:51.660
It reads the cheap file names to find candidates, and then it spends a tiny bit of computational

25:51.660 --> 25:56.580
cost to open only those candidates to check the header line inside, just to confirm the

25:56.580 --> 25:57.460
file name isn't lying.

25:57.900 --> 26:01.300
The findings from this 60-item simulation are profound.

26:01.980 --> 26:06.200
First of all, the savings are entirely concentrated in narrow queries.

26:06.900 --> 26:07.580
Meaning what?

26:07.580 --> 26:11.360
If you ask a broad question like, what can I work on today?

26:12.020 --> 26:18.080
The indexed strategy still has to open like 48 out of 60 files because so many things are

26:18.080 --> 26:19.260
potentially workable.

26:20.000 --> 26:22.060
So the savings aren't great for broad questions?

26:22.600 --> 26:22.840
No.

26:23.520 --> 26:28.660
But if you ask a narrow query like, what specifically needs checking by me right now?

26:28.920 --> 26:30.440
It's a completely different story.

26:30.440 --> 26:34.440
The data shows that narrow queries are 7 to 10 times cheaper.

26:34.880 --> 26:38.840
Instead of opening 60 files, the indexed strategy only opens 6 to 9 files.

26:39.440 --> 26:45.840
That is a massive reduction in friction, especially if you are paying an API cost for an LLM to

26:45.840 --> 26:46.620
read those files.

26:47.240 --> 26:48.040
Oh, good point.

26:48.220 --> 26:49.380
API calls aren't free.

26:49.980 --> 26:55.800
But the simulation also exposed the lethal dangers of this system, specifically when you introduce

26:55.800 --> 26:58.160
a 20% staleness rate into the names.

26:58.160 --> 27:02.000
Okay, let's go back to those lagging names, the ones that time travel backwards.

27:02.540 --> 27:07.980
The simulation found that if 20% of your file names lag behind reality, you will permanently

27:07.980 --> 27:09.840
miss 20% of your work.

27:10.140 --> 27:13.400
Because even the smart indexed strategy won't find them.

27:13.580 --> 27:13.860
Right.

27:14.200 --> 27:19.680
It's looking for the word verify on the nearest surface, but the file is still named experiment.

27:20.100 --> 27:21.460
It just glosses right over it.

27:21.460 --> 27:27.280
So the foundational rule that emerges from this math is renaming must be part of the

27:27.280 --> 27:29.220
actual transition, not a separate chore.

27:29.560 --> 27:29.780
Yes.

27:30.120 --> 27:34.600
You can't say, I'll run the database migration now, and I'll rename the file on Friday when

27:34.600 --> 27:35.700
I clean up my desktop.

27:36.180 --> 27:39.680
The physical act of renaming is the act of transitioning the work.

27:40.180 --> 27:40.660
Exactly.

27:41.360 --> 27:41.620
Okay.

27:41.720 --> 27:44.680
But what about the leading names, the ones that time travel forwards?

27:45.020 --> 27:46.920
This is where it gets super dangerous.

27:46.920 --> 27:52.900
In the simulation, when 20% of the names lead reality, the trust strategy, the one that

27:52.900 --> 27:56.640
blindly trusts file names without checking headers, starts hallucinating.

27:57.220 --> 27:57.920
Hallucinating how?

27:58.320 --> 28:03.480
It acts on about 1.2 items per query that the file name says are ready to build on, but

28:03.480 --> 28:06.920
the reality inside the file is that they haven't been checked yet.

28:07.180 --> 28:11.700
It's like merging a pull request into your production code base simply because the developer

28:11.700 --> 28:16.000
named the branch bug-fixed-final without actually running the test suite to see if the code

28:16.000 --> 28:16.440
compiles.

28:16.880 --> 28:18.560
That is the perfect metaphor.

28:19.560 --> 28:22.520
You are trusting the label over the canonical truth.

28:22.860 --> 28:24.820
The branch name is the cheapest surface.

28:25.000 --> 28:27.040
The test suite is the canonical truth.

28:27.380 --> 28:27.740
Yes.

28:28.200 --> 28:33.520
The indexed strategy means you see the branch name, but before you hit merge, you make one

28:33.520 --> 28:35.680
quick check to confirm the test's past.

28:35.740 --> 28:40.460
It costs a tiny bit more effort, but it prevents taking down the entire production server.

28:40.460 --> 28:47.700
Blindly trusting a leading file name is exactly how an AI agent hallucinates an entire multi-stage

28:47.700 --> 28:50.640
project based on unverified garbage data.

28:51.200 --> 28:52.560
You must confirm the header.

28:52.800 --> 28:56.860
Which brings us to a deep computer science concept that Flyxion applies to our everyday

28:56.860 --> 29:01.760
folders, and that is the fundamental difference between coherence and truth.

29:01.980 --> 29:08.200
To understand this, we really have to recognize what a file name actually is in this ecosystem.

29:08.440 --> 29:08.860
What is it?

29:08.860 --> 29:12.360
The file name is not the canonical state of the work.

29:12.860 --> 29:18.120
The actual text, the data, the evidence inside the body of the file, that is the canonical

29:18.120 --> 29:18.560
state.

29:18.860 --> 29:20.140
So what's the file name?

29:20.440 --> 29:23.380
The file name is just a deliberately denormalized copy.

29:23.820 --> 29:26.380
In computer science terms, it's a cache.

29:27.180 --> 29:27.760
A cache.

29:28.260 --> 29:30.540
We use caches all the time to speed things up, right?

29:30.540 --> 29:37.020
But cache invalidation, keeping the cache synced with the main database, is famously one of the

29:37.020 --> 29:38.260
hardest problems in programming.

29:38.260 --> 29:39.260
Precisely.

29:39.260 --> 29:45.460
And because the file name is a cache, it introduces the central invariant of this entire system.

29:45.460 --> 29:46.960
It's an equation.

29:46.960 --> 29:47.960
Okay.

29:47.960 --> 29:48.960
Let's hear the math.

29:48.960 --> 29:52.760
The equation is n of x equals h of x.

29:52.760 --> 29:53.760
Okay.

29:53.760 --> 29:58.060
So n of x is simply the status written in the file name, the name status.

29:58.060 --> 29:59.060
Yes.

29:59.060 --> 30:03.060
And h of x is the status written in the header inside the document.

30:03.060 --> 30:03.760
Correct.

30:03.760 --> 30:09.880
So the equation n of x equals h of x just means the outside matches the inside.

30:09.880 --> 30:13.580
The file name says verify and the first line inside says verify.

30:13.580 --> 30:14.580
Yes.

30:14.580 --> 30:18.280
And that is the exact trap Flyxion warns about.

30:18.280 --> 30:20.780
This equality does not prove the work is actually done.

30:20.780 --> 30:21.780
It does not prove truth.

30:21.780 --> 30:22.780
It does not prove truth.

30:22.780 --> 30:23.780
It only proves coherence.

30:23.780 --> 30:24.780
Exactly.

30:24.780 --> 30:26.980
Let's pull those apart.

30:26.980 --> 30:27.980
Coherence versus truth.

30:27.980 --> 30:28.980
Go for it.

30:28.980 --> 30:33.980
Coherence just means your filing system isn't structurally broken.

30:33.980 --> 30:42.980
If you mistakenly label a terrible failing experiment as extend in both the file name and the header, the system is perfectly coherent.

30:42.980 --> 30:43.980
The outside matches the inside.

30:43.980 --> 30:44.980
The cache is valid.

30:44.980 --> 30:46.980
But it is entirely untruthful.

30:46.980 --> 30:48.980
The work shouldn't be extended.

30:48.980 --> 30:53.980
Verification, truth, is a claim about the actual work.

30:53.980 --> 30:58.980
Coherence is just a claim about how you organize the representation of the work.

30:58.980 --> 30:59.980
Wow.

30:59.980 --> 31:02.980
So a perfectly coherent file can still be a complete lie.

31:02.980 --> 31:03.980
Yes.

31:03.980 --> 31:10.980
And because coherence can drift, because, you know, humans forget to update headers, you have to run audits.

31:10.980 --> 31:15.980
You have to write a script to check if n x still equals h x across your whole drive.

31:15.980 --> 31:18.980
And Flyxion doesn't just say audit sometimes.

31:18.980 --> 31:27.980
They provide a mathematical formula to calculate exactly how often you should run an audit on your entire directory to find hidden, lagging files.

31:27.980 --> 31:30.980
I didn't realize I'd need algebra to organize my folders.

31:30.980 --> 31:33.980
It's actually a really elegant equation.

31:33.980 --> 31:37.980
It's k equals square root 2n lambda l.

31:37.980 --> 31:38.980
Okay.

31:38.980 --> 31:40.980
Let's break this equation down into human reality.

31:40.980 --> 31:42.980
We have n, which is the size of your project.

31:42.980 --> 31:43.980
Right.

31:43.980 --> 31:45.980
We have lambda, which is the expected rate of mistakes.

31:45.980 --> 31:46.980
Yes.

31:46.980 --> 31:48.980
And l, the cost of a loss.

31:48.980 --> 31:53.980
Let's run three real-world scenarios to see how this math actually changes our behavior.

31:53.980 --> 31:54.980
Okay.

31:54.980 --> 31:55.980
Let's start with scenario one.

31:55.980 --> 31:59.980
You are a solo developer building an iOS app on weekends.

31:59.980 --> 32:00.980
Okay.

32:00.980 --> 32:03.980
Your n, the size of your project, is small, maybe 100 files.

32:03.980 --> 32:07.980
Your lambda, the error rate, is low because it's just you.

32:07.980 --> 32:09.980
You know your own habits.

32:09.980 --> 32:12.980
Let's say you forget to rename a file once every 50 sessions.

32:12.980 --> 32:13.980
Sure.

32:13.980 --> 32:16.980
And your l, the cost of a loss, is tiny.

32:16.980 --> 32:23.980
If you accidentally build a UI component on top of an unverified database schema, you just lose a Saturday afternoon rewriting it.

32:23.980 --> 32:25.980
So what does the formula output?

32:25.980 --> 32:32.980
You plug those small numbers in, and the optimal audit interval, k, comes out to something like 60 sessions.

32:32.980 --> 32:35.980
You only need to run a full audit of your directory every couple of months.

32:35.980 --> 32:41.980
Oh, so the math gives you permission to be lazy because the stakes are low and the environment is contained.

32:41.980 --> 32:42.980
Exactly.

32:42.980 --> 32:44.980
But what about scenario two?

32:44.980 --> 32:48.980
You are a small startup team of five people shipping B2B SaaS features.

32:48.980 --> 32:49.980
Now the math changes.

32:49.980 --> 32:53.980
N is larger, maybe a thousand files.

32:53.980 --> 32:58.980
Lambda goes up because five people are constantly stepping on each other's toes and forgetting to update headers.

32:58.980 --> 33:00.980
And L is higher, right?

33:00.980 --> 33:03.980
A mistake might mean shipping a bug that really annoys a paying client.

33:03.980 --> 33:04.980
Yes.

33:04.980 --> 33:08.980
So the square root of 2N lambda L forces K down.

33:08.980 --> 33:15.980
You probably need to run the berm stale command at the end of every single week to catch incoherence before it metastasizes.

33:15.980 --> 33:16.980
Makes sense.

33:16.980 --> 33:18.980
And scenario three.

33:18.980 --> 33:22.980
A massive enterprise, or like a pharmaceutical research lab.

33:22.980 --> 33:24.980
The math completely flips here.

33:24.980 --> 33:26.980
N is massive.

33:26.980 --> 33:28.980
Tens of thousands of data files.

33:28.980 --> 33:32.980
Lambda is high because you have 500 researchers generating files.

33:32.980 --> 33:36.980
And L, the cost of loss, is catastrophic.

33:36.980 --> 33:37.980
Exactly.

33:37.980 --> 33:47.980
If a data engineer builds a clinical trial report on unverified trial data because a file was wrongly named Extend, you submit flawed data to the FDA.

33:47.980 --> 33:49.980
The loss is measured in billions of dollars.

33:49.980 --> 33:52.980
So the formula forces K to shrink dramatically.

33:52.980 --> 33:55.980
You must audit constantly.

33:55.980 --> 33:59.980
You might need to run the coherence check automatically every single hour.

33:59.980 --> 34:04.980
Or even block any action that hasn't been re-verified in the last 10 minutes.

34:04.980 --> 34:11.980
So it is literally a mathematically precise calibration of paranoia based on the exact stakes of your project.

34:11.980 --> 34:12.980
That's a great way to put it.

34:12.980 --> 34:17.980
Now, so far, we've talked about this as if you are working alone or in a perfectly synchronized team.

34:17.980 --> 34:22.980
